WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.605
Your build system must compile libraries before the programs that use them. Topological sort gives a valid order, or tells you there is a cycle.

00:00:11.805 --> 00:00:12.734
Pause and think.

00:00:13.134 --> 00:00:15.746
How does Kahn's algorithm detect a cycle?

00:00:16.146 --> 00:00:23.460
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.349
Topological sort puts tasks in an order where every task comes after the tasks it depends on.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:40.975
New word: topological sort. An ordering of tasks so every task comes after the tasks it depends on. Picture it like this: Getting dressed: socks before shoes.

00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:45.092
Think about getting dressed.

00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:50.163
Socks before shoes, underwear before trousers, shirt before tie.

00:00:50.613 --> 00:00:53.887
Any order that respects all of these arrows is fine.

00:00:54.337 --> 00:01:00.827
If the rules were shoes before socks and socks before shoes, there would be no valid order. That is a cycle.

00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:07.169
Kahn's algorithm counts incoming arrows for each node. That count is the in degree.

00:01:07.258 --> 00:01:16.906
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:01:18.508 --> 00:01:22.154
Every node with in degree zero is ready, so put it in a queue.

00:01:22.308 --> 00:01:28.612
New word: queue. First-in-first-out structure. Picture it like this: A line at a bus stop.

00:01:30.258 --> 00:01:34.519
Take one out, add it to the order, and remove its outgoing arrows.

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:37.825
Any neighbor whose count drops to zero becomes ready.

00:01:39.175 --> 00:01:40.591
How to detect a cycle.

00:01:41.041 --> 00:01:46.428
If the output order has fewer nodes than the graph, some nodes never reached zero.

00:01:46.557 --> 00:01:53.686
New word: graph. A set of nodes connected by edges. Picture it like this: A map of cities and roads.

00:01:55.307 --> 00:01:57.304
Those nodes are stuck in a cycle.

00:01:57.754 --> 00:02:02.143
This is exactly the course schedule problem: can you finish all courses?

00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:09.971
Count in degrees, start from zeros, peel layer by layer. Fewer outputs than nodes means a cycle.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.214
Back to the question.

00:02:11.614 --> 00:02:14.226
How does Kahn's algorithm detect a cycle?

00:02:14.626 --> 00:02:18.643
If it finishes with fewer nodes output than exist, a cycle remains.

00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:20.714
Quick recap.

00:02:21.114 --> 00:02:23.552
Edges point from prerequisite to dependent.

00:02:23.952 --> 00:02:26.413
Start with nodes that have no prerequisites.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.497
Leftovers mean a cycle.
